  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The analysis relies on mobile phone mobility data as a proxy for tracking student-school relationships, which may introduce representation biases.",
      "what": "This study analyzes the residence-to-school travel patterns of over one million students in Chinese megacities using mobile phone population mobility data. It measures how school quality, school level, and urban expansion affect student segregation and travel distances across different income groups.",
      "why": "The research provides empirical proof that urban expansion and increased school provisioning fail to resolve educational disparities, instead reproducing existing inequalities and segregation. It also demonstrates that the transition from proximity-based districting in primary schools to merit-based admission in high schools decreases income-based segregation."
    }
